{"v":1,"accession":"PRJNA526519","title":"Bacterial community analysis of four wastewater treatment plants from Western Australia","description":"Recycled wastewater has the potential to carry microbial pathogens, such as viruses, bacteria, protozoa and helminths, which can be a major risk to public health. Therefore, wastewater treatment strategies must effectively eliminate pathogens before recycled wastewater is used to supplement drinking and agricultural water supplies. This study aimed to characterise the bacterial composition of four wastewater treatment plants (WWTPs) (three waste stabilisation ponds and one oxidation ditch WWTP using activated sludge treatment) in Western Australia. The hypervariable region 4 (V4) of the bacterial 16S rRNA (16S) gene was sequenced using next-generation sequencing (NGS) on the Illumina MiSeq platform. Sequences were pre-processed in USEARCH v10.0 and denoised into zero-radius taxonomic units (ZOTUs) with UNOISE3. Taxonomy was assigned to the ZOTUs using QIIME 2 and the Greengenes database and cross-checked with the National Center for Biotechnology Information non-redundant nucleotide (NCBI nr/nt) database. Bacterial composition of all WWTPs and treatment stages (influent, intermediate and effluent) were dominated by Proteobacteria (29.0-87.4%), particularly Betaproteobacteria (9.0-53.5%) and Gammaproteobacteria (8.6-34.6%). Nitrifying bacteria (Nitrospira spp.) were found only in the intermediate and effluent of the oxidation ditch WWTP, and denitrifying and floc-forming bacteria were detected in all WWTPs, particularly from the families Comamonadaceae and Rhodocyclales. Twelve pathogens were assigned taxonomy by the Greengenes database, but comparison of sequences from genera and families known to contain pathogens to the NCBI nr/nt database showed that only three pathogens (Arcobacter venerupis, Laribacter hongkongensis and Neisseria canis) could be identified in the dataset at the V4 region. Importantly, Enterobacteriaceae genera could not be differentiated. Family level taxa assigned by the Greengenes database agreed with NCBI nr/nt in most cases, however, BLAST analyses revealed erroneous taxa in the Greengenes database. This study highlights the importance of validating taxonomy of NGS sequences with the NCBI nr/nt database, and recommends including the V3 region of 16S in future NGS studies that aim to identify bacterial enteric pathogens.","center":"Murdoch University","data_type":"Raw sequence reads","submission_date":"2019/03/11 00:00","sample_cols":["accession","title","tier","context","collection_date","collection_year","country","region","place_id","place","location_status","lat","lon","submitted_location","isolation_source","env_broad_scale","env_local_scale","env_medium","eligibility_reason"],"samples":[["SAMN11101159","MIMARKS Survey related sample from wastewater metagenome","probable_urban","municipal_wastewater",null,2015,null,null,null,null,"unresolved",null,null,null,null,"Extraction control 1",null,null,"urban context stated at BioProject level only"],["SAMN11101160","MIMARKS Survey related sample from wastewater metagenome","probable_urban","municipal_wastewater",null,2015,null,null,null,null,"unresolved",null,null,null,null,"Extraction control 2",null,null,"urban context stated at BioProject level only"],["SAMN11101161","MIMARKS Survey related sample from wastewater metagenome","probable_urban","municipal_wastewater",null,2015,null,null,null,null,"unresolved",null,null,null,null,"Extraction control 3",null,null,"urban context stated at BioProject level only"],["SAMN11101162","MIMARKS Survey related sample from wastewater metagenome","probable_urban","municipal_wastewater",null,2015,null,null,null,null,"unresolved",null,null,null,null,"Extraction control 4",null,null,"urban context stated at BioProject level only"],["SAMN11101163","MIMARKS Survey related sample from wastewater metagenome","probable_urban","municipal_wastewater",null,2015,null,null,null,null,"unr